**Proposition 7.1 (finite verification without an all-size cutoff).**
Fix one graph, positive algebraic $A,B$, rational exponents
$\theta_v\ge0$, positive algebraic reference entries, tables and
scalar. Feasibility of (WBF.7), and the comparison $F\le C$ for
a positive algebraic $C$, are decidable by finite algebraic checks.
The global majorant uses at most $2^{2n}$ same-assignment
inequalities. This assertion supplies neither a size cutoff nor
optimizer-cofinality.

Proof. Check that each binary reference sums to one, that entries
are positive and that the rational incidence inequalities hold.
There are finitely many common assignments, and each side of their
majorant is algebraic. For positive $\theta_v$, both
$1/\theta_v$ and $\theta_v$ are rational. Rational powers of
positive real algebraic numbers have unique positive algebraic
values, whose comparisons are decidable; finite sums and products
remain algebraic. At exponent zero take the maximum of the sixteen
algebraic entries. This computes (WBF.8) and decides its comparison
with $C$. The number of assignments is $2^{|E|}=2^{2n}$.
No claim about any unexamined graph or larger template follows
from this finite verification alone. $\square$

